        <tbody>
          {roles.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" class="k">{row.role}</th>
              <td data-col="provider and model" class="k">{row.agent}</td>
              <td data-col="access">{row.access}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </Table>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>enforced by</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("scripts/review-feature.sh")}><code>review-feature.sh</code></a> (provider independence), <a href={repoFile("scripts/lib/review-data.sh")}><code>scripts/lib/review-data.sh</code></a> (findings across rounds)</dd>
        <dt>docs</dt><dd><a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#review-the-implementation")}>Review the implementation</a>, <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#decide-what-to-do-with-review-findings")}>Decide what to do with review findings</a></dd>
        <dt>built in</dt><dd><a href={pull(12)}>PR #12</a> and <a href={pull(14)}>#14</a> (triage), <a href={pull(164)}>#164</a> (your decisions), <a href={pull(222)}>#222</a> (earlier rounds)</dd>
      </dl>
    </details>
  </Section>

  <Section id="risk" title={feature.risk} level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note"><strong>Default</strong>: every change gets a level from the files it changes. A lighter process for low risk needs configuration. <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a> in the docs.</Fragment>
    <dl class="pitch">
      <dt>The problem</dt><dd>If a typo fix and a change to your project's rules get the same process, one gets too much and the other too little.</dd>
      <dt>What you get</dt><dd>Effort goes where the risk is. You can raise a level, no AI tool lowers it, and high risk always keeps the full review.</dd>
      <dt>Illustrative example</dt><dd>With the lighter process, a fix in the documentation can skip review and then asks for your approval. Changing an existing attendance test makes a change high risk.</dd>
    </dl>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>How the risk level is set: files, settings and a real record</summary>
      <p><b>What Codeyard does.</b> A script gives every feature a risk level, low, medium or high, from the files it changes; you can raise it, and no agent lowers it. The level picks the model and how strict the review rounds are. Under <code>profile: light</code>, or with <code>low: review: none</code>, a low-risk change can go without review; its merge then needs your approval.</p>
      <figure class="plain">
        <svg class="lines" viewBox="0 0 1100 160" role="img" aria-labelledby="risk-t" aria-describedby="risk-words">
          <title id="risk-t">How the level is set: the classes of the changed paths, the escalations and your hint each give a level; the highest counts and nothing lowers it. The review can raise it again: an impact on the architecture classified as major or breaking makes the feature high.</title>
          <path class="branch" d="M1000 80 L960 40 H800 L760 80"/>
          <text class="note" x="880" y="28" text-anchor="middle">major or breaking impact: high</text>
          <path class="l2" d="M80 80 H1000"/>
          <circle class="dot" cx="80" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="80" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Changed paths</text><text class="sub k" x="80" y="137" text-anchor="middle">risk.conf</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="300" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="300" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Escalations</text><text class="sub" x="300" y="137" text-anchor="middle">tests, check lists, policies</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="530" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="530" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Your hint</text><text class="sub k" x="530" y="137" text-anchor="middle">risk: high | --risk</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="760" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="760" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Level</text><text class="sub" x="760" y="137" text-anchor="middle">the highest; never lowered</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="1000" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="1000" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Review</text><text class="sub" x="1000" y="137" text-anchor="middle">impact on the architecture</text>
        </svg>
        <ol class="words" id="risk-words">
          <li><b>Changed paths</b><small class="k">.agents/policies/risk.conf</small><p class="cond">The highest class of any changed path; a path no class names gets the default.</p></li>
          <li><b>Escalations</b><small>tests, check lists, fixtures, manifests, policies, prompts, schemas</small><p class="cond">A changed existing one makes the feature high.</p></li>
          <li><b>Your hint</b><small class="k">risk: high | risk: medium | --risk</small><p class="cond">Before the first session.</p></li>
          <li><b>Level</b><small>the highest counts; nothing lowers it</small><p class="cond">It picks the model of each writing role and what the review rounds require.</p></li>
          <li><b>Review</b><small>classifies the impact on the architecture</small><p class="cond">Major or breaking makes the feature high.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the description of pull request 249"><span class="src">{bare(pull(249))} · excerpt</span>Risk: medium
- website/src/pages/about.astro: no class of .agents/policies/risk.conf names it; the default is medium
[...]
- profile: none

Triage mode: full (the default; .agents/policies/risk.conf of the base has no 'medium: triage:' line)</pre>
      <Table>
        <caption>The sources of the level; the highest one counts. From <code>scripts/lib/risk.sh</code> and the header of <code>risk.conf</code>, read from the base branch, so a feature cannot relax the rules it is classified by.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">source</th><th scope="col">what raises the level</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Paths</th><td data-col="what raises the level">The highest class of any changed path; a path no class names gets the default. As shipped: default medium, the workflow's own gate files high, no low class.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Escalations</th><td data-col="what raises the level">Always high: a changed or removed line of an existing test or of the check lists, any change to a shared test fixture, a changed existing manifest, and a changed existing file under <code>.agents/policies/</code>, <code>.agents/prompts/</code> or <code>.agents/schemas/</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">The reviewer</th><td data-col="what raises the level">An impact on the architecture classified as major or breaking.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">You</th><td data-col="what raises the level">The Issue label <code>risk: high</code> or <code>risk: medium</code>, or <code>--risk</code>, before the first session.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </Table>
      <p>Any source can raise the level; nothing lowers it. The level, the workflow profile and the agents' permissions are three separate settings: the level describes the change, the profile how much process the project wants for low and medium risk, and the permissions follow the role (implementers write, reviewers only read, unattended sessions ask nothing).</p>
      <Table matrix>
        <caption>What each level gets under each profile. Under both profiles every commit needs the full verification and no script merges. The light column shows what changes from standard; a project without a profile line keeps the policy it has.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">level</th><th scope="col">standard</th><th scope="col">light</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl low">low</span></th>
            <td data-col="standard"><p>Reviewed by another provider; full triage.</p><p>As shipped there is no low class: changes start at medium.</p></td>
            <td data-col="light" class="changed"><p><span class="chg">changed</span> Documentation and process files are low. No review: the commit and the pull request say so, and ask for your approval of the merge.</p></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl medium">medium</span></th>
            <td data-col="standard"><p>Reviewed; full triage.</p><p>Implementer: Claude opus.</p></td>
            <td data-col="light" class="changed"><p><span class="chg">changed</span> Reviewed; light triage: minor findings and suggestions can be accepted or deferred without a fix round.</p></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row"><span class="lvl high">high</span></th>
            <td data-col="standard"><p>Reviewed; full triage.</p><p>Implementer: Claude fable.</p></td>
            <td data-col="light"><p>Unchanged: reviewed, full triage, the stronger model. No profile can remove these.</p></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </Table>
      <p>Without a profile, a project can set the same exceptions one by one in <code>risk.conf</code>: a <code>low:</code> class of its own paths, and <code>low: review: none</code>, which lets a low feature go without review; its pull request then asks for your approval of the merge. High always keeps every control.</p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>key</dt><dd><code>profile: light | standard</code>; per level, <code>&lt;level&gt;: verification | review | triage: &lt;value&gt;</code></dd>
        <dt>model per level</dt><dd><code>implementer@high: claude fable</code> in <a href={repoFile(".agents/agents.conf")}><code>agents.conf</code></a></dd>
        <dt>try it</dt><dd><code>./scripts/check-risk.sh</code> in a feature worktree prints the level and its reasons</dd>
        <dt>built in</dt><dd><a href={pull(194)}>PR #194</a> (the level), <a href={pull(202)}>#202</a> and <a href={pull(211)}>#211</a> (models per level), <a href={pull(214)}>#214</a> (profiles), <a href={pull(229)}>#229</a> (your hint)</dd>
      </dl>
    </details>
  </Section>

  <section id="control" class="group" aria-labelledby="control-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="control-h">{group.control}</h2>
      <p class="lede">Work that runs while you are away, and the decisions that stay yours.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Section id="unattended" title={feature.unattended} level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note"><strong>Your choice</strong> per feature; the limits are set by default. <a href={href("docs/detailed-workflow/#run-a-feature-unattended")}>Run a feature unattended</a> in the docs.</Fragment>
    <dl class="pitch">
      <dt>The problem</dt><dd>A tool running while you are away has nobody to answer its questions or to stop it going round in circles.</dd>
      <dt>What you get</dt><dd>The run asks you nothing, and what would need your permission is not granted. It records its decision on each review finding, stops for your decision, a limit or a failure, and never accepts its own change.</dd>
      <dt>Illustrative example</dt><dd>You start the history view in the evening. In the morning there is a proposed change with its checks and review, or one question for you.</dd>
    </dl>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>How an unattended run is limited: files, settings and a real record</summary>
      <p><b>What Codeyard does.</b> One command, <code>run-feature.sh</code>, takes a feature from its Issue to an open pull request without asking you anything, inside fixed limits: anything that would need your permission is denied, sessions, repairs and review rounds are capped, and an agent that needs your decision stops the run instead of guessing.</p>
      <figure class="plain">
        <svg class="lines" viewBox="0 0 1100 160" role="img" aria-labelledby="unattended-t" aria-describedby="unattended-words">
          <title id="unattended-t">An unattended run: run-feature.sh starts the implementer under the unattended profile, verifies, and runs the review rounds, with the fixes of each round, until an open pull request; there the run ends, and you merge. An agent that needs your decision stops the run with status 3.</title>
          <path class="branch" d="M680 80 L640 40 H320 L280 80"/>
          <text class="note" x="480" y="28" text-anchor="middle">FIX_NOW: the fixes, then the next round</text>
          <path class="l2" d="M80 80 H1040"/>
          <circle class="dot" cx="80" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="80" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Start</text><text class="sub k" x="80" y="137" text-anchor="middle">run-feature.sh</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="280" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="280" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Implement</text><text class="sub" x="280" y="137" text-anchor="middle">at most 4 sessions</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="480" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="480" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Verify</text><text class="sub" x="480" y="137" text-anchor="middle">at most 3 repairs</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="680" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="680" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Review</text><text class="sub" x="680" y="137" text-anchor="middle">at most 5 rounds</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="880" cy="80" r="10"/><text class="st" x="880" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Pull request</text><text class="sub" x="880" y="137" text-anchor="middle">the run ends</text>
          <rect class="gate" x="1030" y="70" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 1040 80)"/><text class="st" x="1040" y="117" text-anchor="middle">Merge</text><text class="sub" x="1040" y="137" text-anchor="middle">by you</text>
        </svg>
        <ol class="words" id="unattended-words">
          <li><b>Start</b><small class="k">run-feature.sh</small></li>
          <li><b>Implement</b><small>unattended profile; by default at most 4 sessions</small><p class="cond">BLOCKED: the run exits with status 3; the same command continues after your answer.</p></li>
          <li><b>Verify</b><small>by default at most 3 repairs of a failing verification</small></li>
          <li><b>Review</b><small>read-only; at most 5 rounds</small><p class="cond">FIX_NOW: the fixes, then the next round.</p></li>
          <li><b>Pull request</b><small>open; the run ends here and never removes a worktree</small></li>
          <li class="you"><b>Merge</b><small>by you</small></li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <ul class="rules">
        <li><b>Bounded permissions.</b> A writing agent runs under the <code>unattended</code> profile: anything that would ask is denied, and it gets no MCP servers (Model Context Protocol servers, programs that give an agent extra tools) or apps, the extra tools of your own agent configuration. Reviewers and triage run <code>read-only</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Bounded effort.</b> At most 5 review rounds in a run, and by default 4 implementer sessions, 3 retries of a failing agent call and 3 repairs of a failing verification.</li>
        <li><b>Stops instead of guesses.</b> An agent that needs your decision ends with a <code>BLOCKED:</code> line and the run exits with status 3. Running the same command again continues where the feature stands.</li>
        <li><b>Never merges.</b> The run ends at an open pull request and never removes a worktree.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the run record of feature 241"><span class="src">.agents/records/feature-241.jsonl · run of PR #249, excerpt</span>{record}</pre>
      <p>Every run leaves such a record on your machine: each step and its duration, the agent, model and level, the verification seconds, the findings and why a run stopped. <code>report-runs.sh</code> summarises it per feature and <code>analyze-runs.sh</code> lets a read-only analyst propose changes; <a href={href("examples/#analysis")}>the analysis of 28 October 2026</a> is how Codeyard found what to change in itself.</p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>limits</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("scripts/run-feature.sh")}><code>run-feature.sh</code></a>; profiles in <a href={repoFile("scripts/lib/agent.sh")}><code>scripts/lib/agent.sh</code></a></dd>
        <dt>records</dt><dd><code>.agents/records/feature-&lt;issue&gt;.jsonl</code> in the primary checkout, ignored by Git, format <code>run-record/v1</code> (<a href={`${repoFile("docs/development.md")}#run-records`}><code>docs/development.md</code></a>)</dd>
        <dt>docs</dt><dd><a href={href("docs/technical-reference/#what-is-recorded-on-your-machine")}>What is recorded on your machine</a></dd>
        <dt>built in</dt><dd><a href={pull(136)}>PR #136</a> to <a href={pull(140)}>#140</a> (records), <a href={pull(223)}>#223</a> (stops)</dd>
      </dl>
    </details>
  </Section>

  <Section id="authority" title={feature.authority} level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note"><strong>Default</strong>: no script accepts a change. GitHub enforces your approval only with your repository settings. <a href={href("docs/configuration-and-security/#changes-that-need-the-owner")}>Changes that need the owner</a> in the docs.</Fragment>
    <dl class="pitch">
      <dt>The problem</dt><dd>With AI tools doing the work, it is unclear which decisions are still yours.</dd>
      <dt>What you get</dt><dd>You agree the requirements, approve the plan and accept each change. A change to the project's rules or design says it needs your approval.</dd>
      <dt>Illustrative example</dt><dd>Attendance becomes part of the gym's application only when you accept its change; until then your project stays as it was.</dd>
    </dl>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>How your approval is marked: files, settings and a real record</summary>
      <p><b>What Codeyard does.</b> No script merges: you agree the requirements, approve the plan, answer the questions, and merge. A pull request that changes a protected file, such as an ADR, a CI workflow or the workflow's own gate, is marked as needing your approval; GitHub refuses a merge only as far as the settings of <a href={href("docs/github-setup/")}>GitHub setup</a> make it. Until agents get a GitHub identity of their own (<a href={issue(99)}>#99</a>), an agent has the GitHub rights of the account that started it.</p>
      <figure class="plain">
        <svg class="lines" viewBox="0 0 1100 170" role="img" aria-labelledby="authority-t" aria-describedby="authority-words">
          <title id="authority-t">Your decisions: you agree the requirements and approve the plan; a feature asks you only when it needs a decision; a pull request that changes a protected path, has a major or breaking impact or no review is marked as needing your approval of exactly its commit; and the merge is left to you, no script merges.</title>
          <path class="branch" d="M780 90 L820 50 H970 L1010 90"/>
          <rect class="gate" x="885" y="40" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 895 50)"/>
          <text class="note" x="895" y="24" text-anchor="middle">protected path, major impact or no review</text>
          <path class="l1" d="M80 90 H300"/>
          <path class="l2" d="M300 90 H1010"/>
          <rect class="gate" x="70" y="80" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 80 90)"/><text class="st" x="80" y="127" text-anchor="middle">Requirements</text><text class="sub" x="80" y="147" text-anchor="middle">you agree them</text>
          <rect class="gate" x="290" y="80" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 300 90)"/><text class="st" x="300" y="127" text-anchor="middle">Plan</text><text class="sub k" x="300" y="147" text-anchor="middle">finish-planning.sh</text>
          <rect class="gate-o" x="530" y="80" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 540 90)"/><text class="st" x="540" y="127" text-anchor="middle">Questions</text><text class="sub" x="540" y="147" text-anchor="middle">you answer, when asked</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="780" cy="90" r="10"/><text class="st" x="780" y="127" text-anchor="middle">Pull request</text><text class="sub" x="780" y="147" text-anchor="middle">rules from the base branch</text>
          <rect class="gate" x="1000" y="80" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 1010 90)"/><text class="st" x="1010" y="127" text-anchor="middle">Merge</text><text class="sub" x="1010" y="147" text-anchor="middle">by you</text>
        </svg>
        <ol class="words" id="authority-words">
          <li class="you"><b>Requirements</b><small>you agree them with the Project Grill</small></li>
          <li class="you"><b>Plan</b><small class="k">finish-planning.sh</small></li>
          <li class="ask"><b>Questions</b><small>you answer, only when a feature asks</small></li>
          <li><b>Pull request</b><small>the rules are read from the base branch</small><p class="cond">A protected path, a major or breaking impact, or no review: your approval of exactly this commit.</p></li>
          <li class="you"><b>Merge</b><small>by you; no script merges</small></li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <p>A pull request is marked as needing your approval when a feature changes a protected path, when the reviewer classifies its impact on the architecture as major or breaking, or when there is no review. Always protected: every ADR, the CI workflows, the files that carry out the gate, and a changed or removed line of the check lists. The rules are read from the base branch, so a feature cannot loosen its own gate. The mark is a statement, not a lock: only the repository settings make GitHub enforce it.</p>
      <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the description of pull request 222"><span class="src">{bare(pull(222))} · excerpt</span>Merge approval:
- required from the project owner, for exactly this commit
[...]
- scripts/review-feature.sh: the file defines or carries out the workflow and its gate
- the independent review classified the impact on the architecture as major: [...]</pre>
      <div class="split">
        <div><h5>Codeyard enforces</h5><ul>
          <li>The risk level, and that no agent lowers it</li>
          <li>A reviewer of another provider, read-only</li>
          <li>Verification before review and before commit</li>
          <li>The completion gate and the triage rules</li>
          <li>Rules read from the base branch</li>
        </ul></div>
        <div><h5>Your GitHub settings enforce</h5><ul>
          <li>A ruleset on <code>main</code>, a GitHub setting that requires a pull request and the CI check (the checks GitHub runs on every pull request); <code>doctor.sh</code> warns when it is missing</li>
          <li>Code-owner review, once you replace the placeholder in <a href={repoFile(".github/CODEOWNERS")}><code>CODEOWNERS</code></a></li>
          <li>An approval the agent cannot give itself: needs a separate agent identity, <a href={issue(99)}>not built yet</a></li>
        </ul></div>
      </div>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>checks</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("scripts/check-guardrails.sh")}><code>check-guardrails.sh</code></a> locally, and the Guardrails job of <a href={repoFile(".github/workflows/guardrails.yml")}><code>guardrails.yml</code></a> on every pull request</dd>
        <dt>settings</dt><dd><a href={href("docs/github-setup/")}>GitHub setup</a> in the docs</dd>
        <dt>built in</dt><dd><a href={pull(100)}>PR #100</a> (the gate), <a href={pull(200)}>#200</a> (approval requests and the content tree)</dd>
      </dl>
    </details>
  </Section>

  <Section id="learn" title="Use past runs to improve the process">
    <Fragment slot="note"><strong>Optional</strong>: records are kept by default; the analysis runs when you start it. <a href={`${repoFile("docs/development.md")}#run-records`}>The records in detail</a>.</Fragment>
    <dl class="pitch">
      <dt>The problem</dt><dd>The same problem can cost review rounds in feature after feature without anyone noticing.</dd>
      <dt>What you get</dt><dd>When you start it, a read-only AI reads the records of many runs and proposes changes to your process, each citing its runs. Nothing changes until you decide.</dd>
      <dt>Real example</dt><dd>The analysis of 28 October 2026 found that findings raised again after being settled cost extra triage and repair rounds in five runs. Its proposal became <a href={issue(217)}>Issue #217</a>, carried out in <a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a>.</dd>
    </dl>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>How the analysis works: files, settings and a real record</summary>
      <p><b>What Codeyard does.</b> Every run leaves a record on your machine: the review rounds and their findings, what the triage decided, the checks that failed, why a run stopped, and the time and tokens of each step. When you start it, a read-only analyst reads the records of many features and proposes what to change.</p>
      <figure class="plain">
        <svg class="lines learn" viewBox="0 0 1240 236" role="img" aria-labelledby="learn-t" aria-describedby="learn-words">
          <title id="learn-t">The run records of several features run into one analysis. Its proposals pass your decision, and each goes to its own place with the review that applies there.</title>
          <text class="note" x="110" y="14" text-anchor="middle">the last n features</text>
          <path class="l2" d="M40 30 H200 L280 110"/>
          <path class="l2" d="M40 70 H200 L240 110"/>
          <path class="l2" d="M40 110 H500"/>
          <path class="l2" d="M40 150 H200 L240 110"/>
          <path class="l2" d="M40 190 H200 L280 110"/>
          <circle class="dot" cx="200" cy="30" r="8"/><circle class="dot" cx="200" cy="70" r="8"/><circle class="dot" cx="200" cy="110" r="8"/><circle class="dot" cx="200" cy="150" r="8"/><circle class="dot" cx="200" cy="190" r="8"/>
          <text class="sub" x="200" y="226" text-anchor="middle">run records</text>
          <path class="l1" d="M500 110 H620 L680 50 H900 M620 110 H900 M620 110 L680 170 H900"/>
          <circle class="dot" cx="400" cy="110" r="10"/><text class="st" x="400" y="86" text-anchor="middle">Analysis</text><text class="sub k" x="400" y="142" text-anchor="middle">analyze-runs.sh</text><text class="sub" x="400" y="162" text-anchor="middle">read-only</text>
          <rect class="gate" x="510" y="100" width="20" height="20" transform="rotate(45 520 110)"/><text class="st" x="520" y="86" text-anchor="middle">You decide</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="900" cy="50" r="8"/><text class="sub" x="918" y="55">ADR, instruction, skill, docs</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="900" cy="110" r="8"/><text class="sub" x="918" y="115">a check in a script</text>
          <circle class="dot" cx="900" cy="170" r="8"/><text class="sub" x="918" y="175">an Issue, here or in Codeyard</text>
          <text class="note" x="760" y="226" text-anchor="middle">each with the review that applies there</text>
        </svg>
        <ol class="words lines-words" id="learn-words">
          <li><b>Run records</b><small>of the last n features: rounds, findings, triage, stops, time and tokens</small></li>
          <li><b>Analysis</b><small class="k">analyze-runs.sh --last 10</small><p class="cond">A read-only agent; it changes nothing.</p></li>
          <li class="you"><b>You decide</b></li>
          <li><b>Each proposal in its place</b><small>an ADR, an agent instruction, a skill, the documentation, a check in a script, or an Issue, here or in Codeyard's repository</small><p class="cond">with the review that applies there</p></li>
        </ol>
      </figure>
      <ul class="rules">
        <li><b>A finding needs two runs.</b> One observation stays a hypothesis, and “not enough evidence” is a valid outcome.</li>
        <li><b>Every conclusion cites its records,</b> and says which differences in task size, model and workflow version it considered.</li>
        <li><b>Nothing changes by itself.</b> Each proposal names its place, and you take it through the review and approval there.</li>
        <li><b>Repeatable, and nothing counted twice.</b> Each analysis keeps its input and sources; evidence an earlier analysis used is marked as such.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre role="region" class="excerpt" aria-label="Excerpt of the run analysis of 28 October 2026"><span class="src">.agents/analysis/20261028T181115Z-6036a8954003/report.md · proposal 5, excerpt</span>{analysis}</pre>
      <p>C1, C2 and C17 are conclusions of the same report, each with the runs it rests on. The proposal became <a href={issue(217)}>Issue #217</a> and was merged with <a href={pull(222)}>PR #222</a>.</p>
      <p class="needs">You start an analysis by hand; running it periodically is an open Issue (<a href={issue(131)}>#131</a>).</p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>scripts</dt><dd><a href={repoFile("scripts/analyze-runs.sh")}><code>analyze-runs.sh</code></a>, <a href={repoFile("scripts/report-runs.sh")}><code>report-runs.sh</code></a>, <a href={repoFile("scripts/show-experience.sh")}><code>show-experience.sh</code></a></dd>
        <dt>example</dt><dd><a href={href("examples/#analysis")}>The analysis of 28 October 2026</a>: 41 runs, five Issues</dd>
      </dl>
    </details>
  </Section>
